{"id": 32, "ts": 1780589007.387176, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I\u2019m leaning toward recommending Candidate\u202fB.\u202fJames confirmed we have no concrete evidence of Candidate\u202fD\u2019s teamwork, and the only notes we have describe him as \u201cmoody,\u201d a \u201cloner,\u201d a \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all,\u201d and having a hot temper \u2013 all red flags for crew cooperation and professional communication in a long\u2011haul cockpit.\u202fWhile D does take responsibility seriously and solves problems well, those interpersonal concerns could undermine safety\u2011critical collaboration.\nConversely, Candidate\u202fC still lacks any demonstrated adaptability or willingness to accept feedback, which is essential for continuous learning on international routes.\u202fHis \u201cnot very willing to further his education\u201d suggests limited openness to development, and we have no evidence he can adjust to new procedures or feedback.\nCandidate\u202fB, despite occasional tone issues, shows strong operational reliability (very conscientious), excellent stress resilience, solid weather\u2011assessment judgment, and good technical competence (computer skills).\u202fThose core safety criteria are well\u2011documented, and his interpersonal shortcomings appear less severe than D\u2019s teamwork doubts or C\u2019s adaptability gap.\nTherefore, based on the current evidence, Candidate\u202fB remains the safest overall choice."}
